Description: Add atomic per-joint feedback reception evidence to ArmMotorBus.
 Upstream exposes only a sticky feedbackReady() flag and no reception time, and
 it reads `states()` and the reception evidence as two SEPARATE lock passes. A
 caller therefore cannot (a) tell "receiving continuously" from "stopped after
 the first packet", (b) stamp a state with the time the data actually ARRIVED,
 or (c) get a consistent (value, age) pair.

 This patch adds:
   * steadyNowNs(): one monotonic clock source (std::chrono::steady_clock, same
     family as r1_robot::nowNs()) so ages and reads share one epoch.
   * Per-joint rx bookkeeping (rx_count, last_rx_ns) recorded inside the existing
     feedback writer, under fb_mutex.
   * feedbackStats(): the reception evidence on its own.
   * feedbackSnapshot(): values + reception evidence + a single captured `now`,
     read together under ONE fb_mutex lock, so an old state can never be paired
     with a fresh timestamp.  This is the entry point r1_robot uses for readState.

 The submodule is NEVER modified in place: r1_robot copies these two files into
 the build tree and applies this patch there (cmake/R1SdkDependency.cmake).
 Version: v2 (adds feedbackSnapshot(); v1 added only feedbackStats()).
 Pinned against: unitreerobotics/r1-7a_sdk commit 480cfc8c (VERSION 0.1.0).
--- a/include/arm_motor_bus.h	2026-10-10 16:53:24.994423002 +0800
+++ b/include/arm_motor_bus.h	2026-10-10 16:53:56.072558792 +0800
@@ -105,6 +105,35 @@
     /** True if every joint has received at least one valid feedback. */
     bool allFeedbackReady() const;
 
+    /**
+     * Per-joint feedback reception statistics.
+     *
+     * PATCHED ADDITION (not present upstream).  The upstream class only exposes
+     * a sticky `feedbackReady()` boolean, which cannot distinguish "receiving
+     * continuously" from "stopped receiving after the first packet", and it
+     * exposes no reception timestamp at all.  These counters/timestamps let a
+     * caller detect stale feedback and stamp a state with the time the data
+     * actually arrived instead of the time it was read.
+     */
+    struct FeedbackStats {
+        uint64_t packets = 0;   //!< valid (CRC-checked) packets received
+        bool received = false;  //!< packets > 0
+        uint64_t age_ns = 0;    //!< time since the last valid packet (0 if none)
+        uint64_t now_ns = 0;    //!< monotonic time `age_ns` was measured against
+    };
+    std::array<FeedbackStats, kArmJointCount> feedbackStats() const;
+
+    /**
+     * Feedback values AND their reception evidence, captured together under one
+     * lock (PATCHED ADDITION).  This is the atomic snapshot: a caller must never
+     * pair a value read from one lock pass with an age read from another.
+     */
+    struct FeedbackSnapshot {
+        std::array<ArmJointState, kArmJointCount> states;
+        std::array<FeedbackStats, kArmJointCount> stats;
+    };
+    FeedbackSnapshot feedbackSnapshot() const;
+
 private:
     struct Impl;
     std::unique_ptr<Impl> impl_;
